1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Virginia was the daughter of Vincezo Terranova Vacirca (Nov 26 1886 Chiaramonte, Gulfi Italy-Dec 25/26 1955/56/57 Rome, Italy) and Chiara 'Clara' Maria Maddalena Palumbo* (Sept 5 1883 Puglia, Italy-Sept 18 1962 Old
2 CONC  Forge PA?). Her parents were social activists and journalists. Her sister was Myrtle Palumbo Vacirca Quinn.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the granddaughter of Antonio 'Toto' Vacirca (about 1860 Bari- ?) and Virginia Terranova (1868 Vittoria, Ragusa, Sicilia- 19?). They were married in New York. Antonio was the son of Vincenzo Vacirca and Lucia di Ventura. Lu
2 CONC cia was the daughter of Baron Ventura (barone di Ventura). They were all born in Chiaramonte, Sicily.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her maternal grandparents were Olimpia Monticelli della Valle (May 8 1845 Aversa, Caserta, Campania, Italy-Jan 1901) and Consalvo Palumbo (Trani, Bari, Puglia). Her great grandparents were Filippo Della Valle (Oct 2 1801 Napoli, N
2 CONC apoli, Campania-March 2 1885) and Caterina Redding. Filippo was the son of Cesare Della Valle (Feb 9 1777–Feb 27 1860) and Chiara di Tocco Cantelmo Stuart. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Of interest, the line of sucession continued with Filippo's nephew Cesare Monticelli della Valle, the 5th Duke of Ventignani (Jan 14 1824-Feb 15 1900) son of his older brother Francesco Saverio Della Valle Monticelli.  He was marr
2 CONC ied to Cristina Imperali (1840-April 10 1901). Cristina Imperali was the daughter of Carlo Imperali (March 16 1798- Dec 12, 1860), son of Vincenzo, the 5th Prince of Francavilla Sept 3 1738- Sept 3 1816), and Josephine Marullo (bo
2 CONC rn 1818). Cesare's great-grandparents were Cesare Monticelli della Valle and Anna Ninfa Valignano  (daughter of Frederico or Giacomo Valignano, the Marchese of Cephaghetti).
2 CONT
2 CONT Virginia would often speak of her family's castle on the Adriatic Sea in Italy. It is believed to be a castle in her mother's della Valle family. Her maternal great-great grandfather Cesare Della Valle (Feb 9 1777–Feb 27 1860) was
2 CONC  the Duke of Ventignani. Her paternal great-great grandfather (Baron Ventura) held the barone di Ventura in Chiaramonte, Sicily. However, despite her aristocratic ancestry, she and her family worked diligently for democracy and so
2 CONC cial justice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Virginia's first husband was Mark L. Brown (Sept 8 1917-August 9 2002 Bladensburg MD), son of Robert Person Brown (1877 Northampton Co.-1958) and Helen A. Snyder (1885 Mohrsville- 1982) who were married on June 28 1904 in Berks Co
2 CONC unty. Helen's parents were Frances M. Snyder (1849-1919) and Ellen Anna Anderson (1854-1944). Ellen was the daughter of James Anderson and Catharine Himmelberger.
2 CONT
2 CONT Virginia's second husband was Theodore Musial (1925-2009). She was the mother of two sons with Mark L. Brown.
2 CONT
2 CONT *No Pennsylvania death certificate can be located. Perhaps she died somewhere other than the family tree indicates.
