1 NAME Po Robert Jennings /Quinn/
2 GIVN Po Robert Jennings
2 SURN Quinn
2 _MARNM Quinn
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Runnymede Memorial, Englefield Green,															Runnymede Borough,															Surrey,																England
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 20 MAR 1919
2 PLAC Greenville, Greenville County, South Carolina, USA
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 18 JUL 1943
2 PLAC Gibraltar
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [New bio pending. SEE additional information added at bottom showing birth parents.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was an orphan* when he joined the United States Army at age 16, as his widow Myrtle later recounted. That would mean he enlisted about 1935. According to his wife Myrtle's niece, he may have changed his name sometime prior 
2 CONC to his marriage. He became a Freedom Fighter during the Spanish Civil War (1936-1939), and was wounded. Perhaps he left the United States Army before his term of enlistment expired and decided to go fight with Spain (and changed h
2 CONC is name at that time?). When he recovered, he volunteered with the British Royal Air Force to fight the Nazis even before President Franklin Roosevelt declared war. His British military records are under the name Robert Jennings Q
2 CONC uinn.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was shot down over the Mediterranean near Gibraltar. Robert was a gunner  with Squadron 204. The German crew that shot down his Sunderland  was also shot down several months later. During Robert's time with the Royal Air Force,
2 CONC  there was a squadron base at Gibralter. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His birthplace was given on the passenger manifest for the ship Evangeline coming into New York from Hamilton, Bermuda on December 4 1941 (although his name was crossed out). His New York address was 210 West 21st Street and the b
2 CONC irthdate matched.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1940 he was employed as a welder for an aircraft factory and was living with his wife's parents at the same address given on the ship manifest above. He also stated that in 1935 he was residing in Cleveland, Ohio (perhaps an Ar
2 CONC my base?). He said his birthplace was Greenville, South Carolina- just as on the above passenger manifest.
2 CONT
2 CONT The only information originally known about Robert was from military records:
2 CONT
2 CONT ~United States Army veteran
2 CONT ~Freedom Fighter for the Spanish Civil War
2 CONT ~World War II RAF Officer (pilot, officer, air gunner)
2 CONT ~missing in Sunderland JM687
2 CONT ~RAF service number 147229 
2 CONT ~panel 133 on the Runnymede Memorial
2 CONT
2 CONT *No birth record or 1920/1930 census record can be found for a Robert Quinn in South Carolina. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
2 CONT
2 CONT Bio in the Abraham Lincoln Brigade biographical dictionary (courtesy of Findagrave contributor captainlaser):
2 CONT
2 CONT Royce Walker Robertson was a member of the Abraham Lincoln Brigade in the Spanish Civil War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robertson, Royce Walker. (Quinn, Robert J. [real name]); b. March 20, 1919, Greenville, NC; No party affiliation; Received Passport# 494466 on January 22, 1938 which listed his address as 1755 East 19th Street, Cleveland, Ohio; Ar
2 CONC rived in Spain via Agullana on February 15, 1938; Served with an Unidentified unit likely with the XV BDE; WIA July 29, 1938; Deserted on September 1, 1938; Detained at Ripoll on September 3, 1938; Convicted and sentenced to 13 ye
2 CONC ars in prison; Returned to the US on February 26, 1939 aboard the President Roosevelt.
2 CONT Sources: Scope of Soviet Activity; Figueres List; RGASPI. Code A
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that Royce was 19 when he went to Spain and it is likely that his "desertion" was him trying to get out of Spain after the Battle of the Ebro. The group on that ship return page were men who were given certificates of identit
2 CONC y by the US consulate as their passports were never returned to them (the people on lines 4-11 of Page 4 of the Roosevelt return list were Lincolns).
2 CONT
2 CONT Additional info from Findagrave contributor captainlaser:
2 CONT
2 CONT Royce Robertson was the son of Cleveland Young Robertson and Lillie Mae Jennings of Greenville SC. Cleveland Robertson died in 1923 and since Royce was born in 1919 and was the youngest of 8 children born in 13 years, it is likely
2 CONC  he was given away for adoption. Royce used Robertson when fighting in Spain and he returned February 26, 1939 on the President Roosevelt. That ship is misindexed on Ancestry and he would be easy to miss. He is on line 11 of Page 
2 CONC 4 of the Roosevelt return record.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/15261672/robert_jennings-quinn Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Nov 6 2025, 4:50:05 UTC''
